// From the law: Article 7 of the Constitution and Article 2 of the Ordinance of 1881, two
// equal horizontal bands, red above and white below.
// From elsewhere: 4:5 is the annexed model as FOTW's contributors report it. The red is
// the Album des Pavillons 2023's, as FOTW reports it.
vexyl 1
meta {
  name "Monaco"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  license "CC-BY-SA-4.0"
  render-license "CC0-1.0"
  source "https://crteducazione.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/MCO_Constitution_1962_FR.pdf"
  source "https://legimonaco.mc/tnc/ordonnance/1881/04-04-L000701@1881.04.05/"
  source "https://www.crwflags.com/fotw/flags/mc.html"
  note "Article 3 of the Ordinance requires the flag to follow an annexed model, but the annex wasn't found. The 4:5 is what FOTW's contributors report from it, so it isn't read from the law. FOTW also reports that flags are usually made at 2:3, without any rule for that."
  note "The texts give no shade. The red's hex, Pantone and CMYK values are the Album des Pavillons 2023's, as reported by FOTW. White is plain white."
  note "The Constitution was read in a copy on a third-party site, which is headed as the text of 1962 and carries the wording FOTW quotes for the text as amended in 2002."
  law "Constitution of the Principality of Monaco, 17 December 1962, Article 7" {
    source "https://crteducazione.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/MCO_Constitution_1962_FR.pdf"
  }
  law "Sovereign Ordinance of 4 April 1881 on the princely flag and the national flag" {
    source "https://legimonaco.mc/tnc/ordonnance/1881/04-04-L000701@1881.04.05/"
  }
}
palette {
  red "#d21034" {
    pantone "186 C"
    cmyk 10 100 74 2
  }
  white "#ffffff"
}
proportion hoist=4 fly=5

bars red white
